## By the Numbers: A Rising Tide of Recalls

The world of food safety is not for the weak of heart, with 509 recalls already this year, a figure that should give anyone pause before biting into their lunchtime snack. Alarmingly, 152 of these recalls have been classified as Class I, indicating a reasonable probability that exposure to the product will cause serious adverse health consequences or death. That's nearly 30% of all recalls this year--a sharp reminder of the critical importance of vigilance in both production and consumption.

## Recent Developments: A Sour Note for Snacks and More

The most recent recalls bring into sharp focus two recurring themes: Salmonella contamination and undeclared allergens. Legacy Bakehouse LLC and Wawa Beverage Company are among those in the spotlight. The former has issued recalls for several types of bagel crisps due to contamination risks from recalled milk powder. Meanwhile, Wawa's diet beverages were found to contain undeclared milk allergens--a significant issue for those with dairy sensitivities.

The case of the contaminated milk powder used by both Legacy Bakehouse and PS Seasoning & Spices, Inc. further underscores the risks posed by a single tainted supply chain link. And let's not forget the Ghirardelli Chocolate Company, whose recall of their Sweet Ground Powder White Chocolate for potential Salmonella contamination adds another layer to our unease.

## The Usual Suspects: Repeat Offenders and Trends

Among the brands topping the recall charts are names like Moonlight Packing Corporation and Albertsons Companies LLC, each with over 10 Class I recalls this year alone. The frequent appearances of certain brands are a stark reminder that some players in the food industry may need to revisit their quality control measures.

Among the 867 recalls noted all-time, this year has seen a particularly troubling uptick in allergen-related issues, accounting for 251 cases--a sobering statistic for the food allergy community. Meanwhile, Salmonella remains a perennial concern, with 156 incidents so far this year.
